00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.181
- Yeah, so.

00:00:49.181 --> 00:00:50.913
- We'll have to talk
about that. That's nice.

00:00:50.913 --> 00:00:51.746
- That's my little sister, yeah.

00:00:51.746 --> 00:00:54.000
In any case, so we've
just made that connection,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:54.833
which is interesting.

00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:56.361
- Wow, yeah.

00:00:56.361 --> 00:00:57.510
- He was a computer science major.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.060
Learned everything he
knows from Mike Fisher here

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.664
in the front row about security, so and.

00:01:03.664 --> 00:01:07.260
- You can blame him for Google's Foibles,

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:08.760
and that's (Jeffrey laughing).

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.690
- So prior to that, he
worked for American Express

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.640
as a VP for Information.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.917
- Same kind of technology, security.

00:01:19.917 --> 00:01:21.413
- Okay, and at Morgan Stanley.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.800
So we're really excited
to hear from him about

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.790
how he's thinking about
security in the sort of new era

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:33.240
of AI systems and how
they're being deployed.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.350
The way that these kind of
call on questions of privacy

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.110
and security as we input our information

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.150
so that we can get
answers to the questions

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.313
we'd like to know the answers to.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.940
Lots of questions needing
to be asked, answered,

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.500
and explored with this, you
know, massive scale compute

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.300
that's going on at Google.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.860
So we're kind of relieved
to know that a friend,

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.203
a child of Yale University is at the helm

00:02:02.203 --> 00:02:04.380
of this bold new effort at Google.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.090
And we're really excited to hear from him.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.240
He's gonna give about, you
know, 25, 30 minutes of remarks,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.590
and then we'll open it up for questions.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.240
So you'll have plenty of time to ask

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.090
and we will address your
questions after that.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.681
- Great, thanks.

00:02:16.681 --> 00:02:17.691
- All right.

00:02:17.691 --> 00:02:18.700
Welcome Royal.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:19.699
- Thanks, Dean.

00:02:19.699 --> 00:02:20.704
And it was great,

00:02:20.704 --> 00:02:21.841
we saw each other not
even a week ago, is it?

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:22.899
Or maybe it's a little over a week.

00:02:22.899 --> 00:02:25.110
It's something like that in California.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.964
I was super impressed with
the collection of Deans.

00:02:28.964 --> 00:02:31.240
How many deans did you have there?

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:32.073
- [Jeffrey] All of them.

00:02:32.073 --> 00:02:33.864
- It really was all of them, yeah.

00:02:33.864 --> 00:02:34.697
(Jeffrey quietly talking)

00:02:34.697 --> 00:02:38.700
And I mean, apart from
that just being impressive,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.040
it is one of the reasons
I've wanted to get closer

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.510
to the university again and really,

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:46.590
as the computer science
department has sort of expanded,

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.721
I mean, that's probably an understatement.

00:02:48.721 --> 00:02:51.780
You know, when I graduated,

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.440
I know if we ask you had to guess,

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.990
there were eight of us in the major.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.180
So it's sort of, it's
like a different era.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.820
I mean, it might as well have
been in the 19th century,

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:03.783
not the 20th.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.230
But to see though

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.780
the full university come together

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.580
around some of these topics.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.570
I don't, you know, as someone
who wants to participate

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:16.740
in these discussions

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.800
and help society work out
a lot of these questions,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.050
I think Yale has an incredibly
important role to play,

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.460
and I would like to, you know,

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:24.960
help in that and be a part of it.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.540
So I really do feel like
the meetings we've had

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.840
over the last year and even
today with students, professors,

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.820
not just in the computer
science department,

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:34.290
I'm super excited for.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:39.290
So as we, I have maybe 15
slides, sort of relatively quick

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.733
and just hopefully set the table.

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:44.160
But as Dean Brock said,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.803
I do privacy, safety, and security,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.610
anything bad that can happen at Google.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.860
And so we could talk about any of that

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:53.940
as you ask questions.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.290
The slides are more about AI

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.270
just 'cause I think it's something that's,

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.390
it's more topical and
sort of front of mind

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:01.470
for me at the moment.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.693
But happy to go in a
variety of directions.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.863
Let's see, with that,

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:14.990
the rendering across the Google
slides instead of PowerPoint

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.540
probably gets that AI in the wrong place.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.972
The first point I'd like to make,

00:04:20.972 --> 00:04:22.890
and as we go through the slides,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:26.550
I think we sometimes forget
that in the security of AI,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.000
so this means think about
like software security,

00:04:30.176 --> 00:04:32.550
data protection, cryptography,

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:33.870
that's a whole body of work,

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.700
and I'll talk about
that in the second half.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.890
But I've been really impressed

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:41.730
and excited about the uses of
these large language models

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:45.540
in AI more generally in the
service of safety and security.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.691
So I'm just gonna remind
us a little bit of that

00:04:48.691 --> 00:04:49.650
in beginning so that in our enthusiasms

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.740
to kind of restrict and regulate.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.170
You know, you see lots of, you
saw the White House this week

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.600
and lots of efforts, and
it's all good efforts

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.850
to come to grips with it.

00:04:59.997 --> 00:05:02.792
But we are doing some
incredible things as a society.

00:05:02.792 --> 00:05:04.470
You can think of the AlphaFold,

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.050
just the most recent
publication this week,

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:09.210
the protein pathways,

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:10.740
like we're going places we've never been.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.370
And I suspect we're really,
really just at the beginning.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:18.270
And those are in the service
of health, safety, even if not,

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:19.800
you know, and I'll talk
about a few examples

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.280
of cybersecurity, but to
me there are two topics.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.850
How do we use this stuff
to make people safer?

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:29.460
And how do we secure
it, make it safe itself?

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:30.933
So I'll cover both of those.

00:05:32.831 --> 00:05:37.020
You know, wildfires, just
a few real world examples.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:38.730
And then we'll get into
the more technical side

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.290
that in California in particular,

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:43.320
obviously in Canada this year.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:48.300
I was in Greece last month
for a democracy forum.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.430
And it tells you kind
of the, I joined Google

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.800
because I felt like it was a moment

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.475
where people would all
of a sudden wanna talk,

00:05:55.475 --> 00:05:56.610
not just about the technical work,

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:57.960
but the implications of it.

00:05:58.835 --> 00:06:00.619
So in Athens, we were talking about

00:06:00.619 --> 00:06:02.850
the role of democracy in technology.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.216
We went with the government there,

00:06:05.216 --> 00:06:07.890
and they're busy in the face
of both floods and fires,

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:10.710
thinking about how they deploy into areas

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:13.680
where there's very little
electricity, little power,

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.480
and even internet connectivity.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:17.730
How do you get very lightweight sensors

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:21.600
so they can detect early
fires and flood conditions

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.370
to get ahead of these things?

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.435
But it's interesting that like the,

00:06:25.435 --> 00:06:27.090
that happened in California,
happening in Greece,

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.753
happening all over the world
for climate related activity.

00:06:30.753 --> 00:06:33.600
And I think enormous
opportunities to use AI there.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.600
The next one, you know,
obviously we talk about Waymo

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.810
or Cruise or these different
examples of self-driving cars,

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:44.810
but the safety benefits
of using large models

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:48.213
in transportation are incredible.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:52.260
Now my brother-in-law
always tells this story

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:56.610
he was a member of the
American Medical Association

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.500
when they convinced the airlines

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:02.670
to let two years and
under children fly on laps

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.700
because they didn't wanna do that

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:06.540
cause they were gonna
kill so many children

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:07.950
in accidents in the flights.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:11.250
And they made the mathematical argument

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.410
that you would save children's lives

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.120
because they wouldn't fly.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.470
They would drive those
200, 300, 400 mile trips,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.770
and if you just did
the actuarial analysis,

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:25.560
you would kill more children under two

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.520
by forcing them to have
a seatbelt on a flight

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.190
than by letting them sit
on their parents' lap.

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:33.150
And just give it an example

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:35.160
of where it's that kind of analysis,

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:36.930
I think we're all gonna have to do

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:39.720
as we think about the
application of these technologies

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:40.650
in different arenas.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:42.930
It doesn't mean that it will
always be the right answer,

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:43.890
but I think you could,

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:45.240
you can make a pretty strong argument

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.130
that the safety benefits,

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:49.140
if we look at it with clear eyes,

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:53.610
will be greater in taking
some risk with the technology

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:55.200
than in being conservative.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:56.760
Again, that's gonna vary,

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.710
but I think it's a really good example.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:04.320
I talked about AlphaFold,

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.320
and I'm most excited about
the developments in health

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:15.000
and, you know, computational
biology and the use of AI.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.830
I actually think it's an area where Yale,

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.920
I'm sort of really excited
to see where you all go,

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:23.220
given the strength of the
molecular biology program here

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:24.060
and the combination

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:27.030
with the sort of the growth
in the technology side.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.920
I mentioned Deep Mind.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:32.550
When you go to the Deep
Mind or the AlphaFold paper.

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:35.970
When you go to the Deep
Mind office in London,

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:40.050
they don't put up any of their
technical papers on the wall,

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:42.450
like sort of in the lobby.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:45.390
They put up only their science papers,

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:49.680
nature, science, health.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.790
They are in this to solve human problems,

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:56.920
not just, you know, it's all tools to them

00:08:58.379 --> 00:08:59.395
to get these things done.

00:08:59.395 --> 00:09:01.053
And I'm super impressed with that group.

00:09:01.053 --> 00:09:03.994
If people have ever heard
Demis Hassabis talk,

00:09:03.994 --> 00:09:05.010
who's the CEO, just incredible.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:07.620
And has become a friend as
all of this has come together,

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:09.360
but I think I'm really inspired

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.863
by the work they do in health.

00:09:11.939 --> 00:09:12.772
And I actually think
it's probably the area

00:09:12.772 --> 00:09:17.400
we'll be most surprised at
the developments using AI.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:21.243
Just one slight sidebar on
that is that my senior thesis,

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.540
we couldn't get David Lerner
to come to this I guess,

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:29.540
but David was my senior thesis advisor

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:32.060
and I've tried to look back in,

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:34.256
you know, my email records are not great

00:09:34.256 --> 00:09:35.763
from the yale.edu era,

00:09:37.139 --> 00:09:39.780
and I need the Google search of that,

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:44.780
but he had a multidimensional
cluster analysis,

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.290
really, I think it's called Diana maybe

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:47.440
or something like that.

00:09:48.335 --> 00:09:50.070
But that very early on they had used

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:55.070
to do mammogram kind of detecting
anomalies in mammograms.

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:57.600
This is, you know, this is 90s,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.790
and now we're, you know, really seeing

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:01.860
the sort of progression in that work.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:05.790
But, you know, way back when,
that's what was going on here,

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:09.330
using neural networks in
their very basic levels.

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:12.783
That's what I started
doing at Yale years ago.

00:10:13.893 --> 00:10:14.843
Super excited about that.

00:10:17.102 --> 00:10:17.935
I mentioned cybersecurity.

00:10:17.935 --> 00:10:20.733
So again, before we even
get into securing AI,

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.240
it's worth noting that, so
the example I have on there

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:26.130
is the Play Store.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:31.130
So there, every day we scan
128 billion app to device pairs

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:38.220
to make sure they're still operating

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.670
in a way that, you know,
we get this sort of safety

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:44.190
that no harmful apps found.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:46.770
And so you get that in
the Android ecosystem.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:51.770
You can't look at 128
billion apps or app instances

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:56.430
without using some sort of
large modeling capabilities.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:59.850
And this goes back further
than the large language models.

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:01.560
But we're using, you know, machines.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.510
Humans are not looking at that stuff.

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:06.780
This is all purely done through
the training of classifiers

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:10.323
and looking for anomalies in
behavior of on-device apps.

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:15.040
Real world making people safer,
you know, across the globe

00:11:15.941 --> 00:11:17.943
at the billions of people scale.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:21.502
Gmail, which people would, you know,

00:11:21.502 --> 00:11:23.638
I think you probably are
more than familiar with,

00:11:23.638 --> 00:11:26.730
and we could take a poll on
whether people are happy or sad

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:28.980
with the spam filters at the moment.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.110
But you know, it's a hard problem

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:34.290
because in cybersecurity we
have the opposite problem

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.142
of some of these other areas

00:11:36.142 --> 00:11:38.553
where you're aiming for a perfect score.

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:41.100
Every time you get to a perfect score,

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.320
and you block all the phishing attacks,

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:45.810
again using these classifiers,

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:48.960
the adversary is sentient and active.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:52.200
They are looking at it and
changing their approach.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:56.370
So there's no such thing
as ever getting to 100%.

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:57.203
You get to 100%,

00:11:57.203 --> 00:12:00.030
they're immediately gonna
change their approach.

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:04.020
Oftentimes even weaponizing
your defenses against you.

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:06.990
So it's in some ways what makes
the field really, really fun

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:09.030
because it's got, it never ends.

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:11.160
It's another thing that
makes it really difficult

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:14.580
because, you know, I get
beaten up semi-regularly

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:18.510
because we let too much spam
in, in trying to tune this.

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:20.719
So it really is like keeping a ship afloat

00:12:20.719 --> 00:12:24.990
to keep it at the right
tolerance for those classifiers

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:27.139
because we're, you know, we're looking at

00:12:27.139 --> 00:12:28.860
literally hundreds of billions of messages

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:30.750
over the course of a week.

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:33.180
The last one, which I
don't think most people

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:34.180
probably know about,

00:12:35.021 --> 00:12:37.050
and by design, and
probably the best example,

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:38.793
something called Safe Browsing.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:44.638
So years ago Google realized
we would learn pretty quickly

00:12:44.638 --> 00:12:47.259
when a phishing site had been stood up

00:12:47.259 --> 00:12:49.090
to be the basis of a email and link

00:12:50.522 --> 00:12:51.778
to get someone to enter a password

00:12:51.778 --> 00:12:52.793
or download some sort of malware.

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:55.320
You know, then watering
hole attacks start to occur

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:57.450
where you're just
browsing, you're searching,

00:12:57.450 --> 00:13:00.013
set up a site to look like
a site you're used to,

00:13:00.013 --> 00:13:03.393
and it drops malware on your local device.

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:05.970
We would pick these things up

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:09.000
just for the scope of what
we saw on the internet

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:11.700
and start to build a list

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.590
and block those things from ever,

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:15.480
those sites from ever being rendering.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:17.840
Or in the case where we weren't sure,

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:18.750
but it looked like it might be a problem,

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.580
we would give a warning.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:22.740
We put that behind Chrome,

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:24.840
but that's now behind Safari.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:27.510
It's behind Firefox, you know,

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:30.270
in some forms it's even
be behind the, you know,

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:35.270
other browsers, and it's
on 4 billion devices.

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:38.459
You know, we're sort of talking
on the order of magnitude

00:13:38.459 --> 00:13:40.038
of the number of people on the planet

00:13:40.038 --> 00:13:41.580
that have access to the internet.

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:43.410
Every time they go to a site,

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:44.820
whether they know it or not,

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:47.880
it's being checked
against these known lists.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:49.470
You know, and we start to offer things

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:52.020
that allow the time between detection

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:55.200
and usage in the browser

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.720
to even go down to under 30 minutes.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.720
So all of that using sort of the

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:03.840
what you'd call old
school machine learning.

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:06.180
This is not necessarily
the large language models,

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.130
but this is what we've
been doing for many years

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:10.230
to solve these kinds of problems at scale.

00:14:13.435 --> 00:14:18.435
So that's using AI to
keep people safe broadly,

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:21.630
physically as well as in
the cybersecurity world.

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:24.270
The next sets of slides are about

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:28.260
how do you make this
newfangled AI infrastructure,

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:32.190
whether it's GPUs and TPUs,
whether it's foundational models

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:35.020
or frontier models that then
are distilled and deployed

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:39.570
into all these other form
factors, chat bots and otherwise,

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:42.330
how do you make that secure by default?

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:45.360
One of the things I love about Google

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:49.090
is we early on decided
we didn't want to put

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.818
too many bells and whistles on the front

00:14:52.818 --> 00:14:53.917
like that safe browsing.

00:14:53.917 --> 00:14:55.290
But you're better off as a user

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:58.110
if you never have to
worry about the security.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:00.960
If we force you to deploy
another piece of software

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:03.270
and configure it, we're likely to fail

00:15:03.270 --> 00:15:05.160
because people are not gonna do it.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:06.990
They're not gonna do it well.

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:10.473
So how do we make this AI
infrastructure secure by default?

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:14.730
And just to give you a taste for the,

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:17.370
kinda the problems that we're up against,

00:15:17.370 --> 00:15:22.370
I talked about the biological sciences.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:26.250
Just to give, we were at
Stanford, I guess I can say that.

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.070
I don't know what the rivalries
are these days on these,

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:32.140
but you know, but in the
spirit of an academic activity

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:34.440
with the computer science department

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:36.870
and the biology department,

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:41.610
and you know, you see these
four base pair primitives

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:45.450
for DNA, just to give it to
the hook to talk about this,

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:48.730
increasingly the 3D printing world

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:51.990
through model organisms if
you wanna think about that,

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:53.730
whether yeast or others,

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:57.570
you can give a computer the instructions,

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:00.360
the DNA to RNA and then protein pathway,

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:01.443
just like AlphaFold,

00:16:02.428 --> 00:16:05.823
instructions to create
life in various forms.

00:16:07.313 --> 00:16:12.313
In fact, the story they told
was, these are sort of the,

00:16:12.816 --> 00:16:17.816
this is Switzerland, Italy,
France in the case of COVID.

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:20.730
So if you think back to sort
of the very earliest months

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:25.110
of '20, this is that, that
says march right here.

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:27.660
And you see that in Italy it was first,

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:32.660
but it, COVID-19 was
transmitted from China

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.910
over the internet in its DNA instructions

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.250
to the WHO in Switzerland

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:43.250
and then printed and existed
in Switzerland in that area

00:16:44.012 --> 00:16:49.012
over 10 days before it arrived
via human transmission.

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:54.570
So when I talk about doing security,

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:57.360
I don't mean it's just
for email or websites.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:01.470
We're headed to a world
where the structures of life

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:03.900
and society are digitized,

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:07.950
and then the whole supply
chain needs to be safe.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:10.350
Cause the mistakes here are very different

00:17:10.350 --> 00:17:12.063
than in one where your email,

00:17:12.915 --> 00:17:15.493
I miss a spam message.

00:17:15.493 --> 00:17:18.810
So it kind of gives you a
sense of where we're headed,

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:21.153
and AI being a really big part of that.

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:27.753
One other idea that we've
talked a little bit about,

00:17:27.753 --> 00:17:31.443
and I think again is a good
one for the context of Yale is,

00:17:33.328 --> 00:17:34.560
let's just start with
the big picture question,

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:35.700
and then explain it.

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:38.433
What is a country on the internet?

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.930
So much of the discussions
that I get involved in are,

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:45.693
well, does the data
resident in the country?

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:49.320
Can the government have
access to this data?

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:54.320
Who, which jurisdiction
has sort of sovereignty

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:56.883
over this question?

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:02.340
And it doesn't really map
to the technical world.

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:03.660
You all know this very well.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:07.140
We've spent the last 50 years
trying to distribute data

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:10.590
and computational power
everywhere it needs to be.

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:12.930
We didn't, it's not really grounded

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:14.913
in the geography of a map.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:18.600
The example that I sometimes talk about,

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:20.850
there's a professor at Claremont

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:25.850
who wrote his dissertation
on the inception

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:29.250
or the sort of the development
of cartographic technology

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:32.250
in the 15th and 16th century

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:34.740
and the establishment of the nation state.

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:37.868
There's, you know, historically
we thought of France

00:18:37.868 --> 00:18:39.180
and England and these sort
of European nation states

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:41.440
coming into form because
of Hobbes and Locke

00:18:42.335 --> 00:18:45.330
and these kind of sort of
theory of how to run a state.

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:47.160
He makes the case

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:50.010
that actually you couldn't have France

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:54.450
Before you could build a very
specific map and replicate it

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:55.860
and distribute it

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:58.893
so that you could even talk
about the thing France.

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:01.080
Just, it's an interesting question,

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:03.810
this sort of intersection
between the material conditions

00:19:03.810 --> 00:19:05.580
or the technology developments

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.480
and the political and societal
structures that go with it.

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:12.540
My question and I think one we
should work on as a community

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:15.090
is what is the nation state?

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:16.920
I get in these weird
discussions with people

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:19.650
about the location of our data center,

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:22.470
and people have religious
discussions about this.

00:19:22.470 --> 00:19:24.420
I get the spirit of it,

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:26.250
but that's just not the way the internet

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:29.250
and applications and software work.

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:32.400
You want these things to
have interconnectivity.

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:36.480
People are not gonna go to the
data center with a forklift

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:39.870
and get the rack and take it away

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:43.830
and then use forensics
software to get the data.

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:46.260
There are much more normal ways

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:49.050
to get at that data if
you wanted to do it.

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:52.080
So I think this discussion
of what is a country

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:54.540
in an era of the internet
is super interesting

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:56.550
and related to then
how we solve questions,

00:19:56.550 --> 00:19:58.860
questions of security, privacy, safety.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:01.693
Because you know, you're gonna have legal

00:20:01.693 --> 00:20:03.773
and regulatory, other
activity on top of that.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:07.890
The other example related to that,

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:09.870
just against some images
that'll recall it,

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:14.095
is I was in Paris, don't tell Lem this,

00:20:14.095 --> 00:20:17.253
my friend in the political
science department,

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:23.230
talking about a new
cybersecurity related law.

00:20:24.933 --> 00:20:28.500
And one of the ministers
said to me, you know,

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:30.630
we are Latin in our sort of history

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:33.000
like so the Latin Roman tradition of law,

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:34.920
and we're addicted to writing laws.

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:36.070
Like we really like it,

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:39.849
which is great, and you know,

00:20:39.849 --> 00:20:42.060
so you get to how people sort
of go after their governance

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:42.893
and things.

00:20:42.893 --> 00:20:44.520
But I said, what will happen

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:49.230
when the laws we continue
to write are now expressed

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:51.363
or written or mapped to COBOL,

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:55.972
and we're 50 years down the road

00:20:55.972 --> 00:20:57.390
and someone needs to change that law.

00:20:57.390 --> 00:20:59.840
You're not gonna be able
to pull it off the shelf

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:05.430
and edit it and publish it
back and have people obey it.

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:08.070
Somebody's gonna have to
know how to change the COBOL.

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:11.175
I just use that as sort
of an antiquated version.

00:21:11.175 --> 00:21:13.560
People even know what COBOL
is here anymore? Okay, good.

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:16.440
Maybe I should use something,
another example for the,

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:17.643
depending on the crowd.

00:21:18.693 --> 00:21:19.526
Fortran, I don't know what the,

00:21:21.351 --> 00:21:23.813
my prologue interpreter written in LISP.

00:21:23.813 --> 00:21:25.710
What was the, that was
my favorite project,

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:30.710
my favorite problem set with,

00:21:30.949 --> 00:21:32.033
I can't remember who had us do that,

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:37.980
but really you in
technology, we in technology

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:42.980
will be coding the laws
of today and tomorrow,

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:49.293
and then changing them will
be a availability risk.

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:51.090
Things will break.

00:21:51.090 --> 00:21:52.323
Let alone will they,

00:21:53.351 --> 00:21:56.460
will the spirit of the law
actually meet the spirit

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:58.470
of the regulation.

00:21:58.470 --> 00:22:00.600
So again, in all these areas of AI,

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:03.360
or I mean AI is gonna make
this even more complicated,

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:05.434
but it just, it happens today

00:22:05.434 --> 00:22:06.784
in the context of software.

00:22:09.115 --> 00:22:12.180
And just to maybe bring that
a little bit closer to home.

00:22:12.180 --> 00:22:14.950
I was in, I've spent a
lot of time in Poland

00:22:14.950 --> 00:22:18.960
over the last 18 months after
the invasion of Ukraine.

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:21.030
And you know, we've gotten

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:23.331
to know the cybersecurity community

00:22:23.331 --> 00:22:24.390
in eastern and central Europe really well

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:26.110
because it's, I mean, the truth is

00:22:27.012 --> 00:22:28.380
that the attacks on
Ukrainian infrastructure

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:29.793
go back to 2014,

00:22:31.516 --> 00:22:34.436
and the Wiper and many of the
kind of destructive malware

00:22:34.436 --> 00:22:37.170
you've seen are a result of
attacks on Ukraine from Russia

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:39.843
and threat actors back at 2016 and beyond.

00:22:42.150 --> 00:22:45.270
The latest developments though

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:46.830
that when I was last in Poland

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:50.910
with the cyber command group
there, you now have weapons.

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:54.600
We saw what was, I think the
Dutch just announced something

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:59.111
about a aircraft that's being, you know,

00:22:59.111 --> 00:22:59.944
that's being delivered.

00:22:59.944 --> 00:23:03.300
But they pointed out to
us that the supply chain

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:08.300
for ships, trucks, warehouses,
for all these weapons

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:12.250
or supplies that are ultimately
flowing through Poland

00:23:13.092 --> 00:23:14.640
and into Ukraine are the,

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:19.050
that's the underbelly of
the cybersecurity issue

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:22.080
because Russian threat actors track that.

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:25.530
If they can figure out
which device, which weapon,

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:30.240
which supply is going where,
when, strategic advantage,

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:31.350
not just in the battlefield,

00:23:31.350 --> 00:23:35.340
but in sort of the broader context of war.

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:38.430
And that's got nothing to do

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:40.680
with the military or the government.

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:43.740
These are like mom and pop warehouses

00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:48.390
and trucking companies who
know nothing about Google

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:52.380
or Morgan Stanley's
level of cybersecurity.

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:53.880
These people are as vulnerable

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:57.480
as anyone on the planet to these attacks.

00:23:57.480 --> 00:24:01.800
I say all of those things then to say

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:03.810
we are working on this framework

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:05.400
called the Secure AI Framework.

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:09.660
And just the spirit of
it is to not throw out

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:13.860
in this new rush to AI
all of the improvements

00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:16.710
and foundational security work

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:19.200
that we've built up over
the last couple decades.

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:22.770
Think of supply chains, think
of kind of the full stack

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:24.450
of a piece of software.

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:25.800
I'm not gonna drain it here

00:24:26.676 --> 00:24:28.080
because it's more of a framework,

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:32.700
but the idea is that
the way we handle data,

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:35.730
the way we write, you know,
the logic, the business logic

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:38.610
of a piece of software, the
way the input is validated,

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:42.930
think in the context of AI,
historically for a website,

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:46.650
you would validate the
entry that a user put in

00:24:46.650 --> 00:24:49.356
before you passed it
along to the database.

00:24:49.356 --> 00:24:50.520
People have heard of
things like SQL injection

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:54.210
or sort of where the code
is passed to the database,

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:56.880
and if not checked, can extract more data

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:58.754
than you would think.

00:24:58.754 --> 00:24:59.880
Sort of simple approach to it.

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:02.771
But in the world of the
large language model,

00:25:02.771 --> 00:25:07.260
there's no intermediation of the language

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:08.880
because the language is the code.

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:13.880
The model interacts with the
English that the user types in.

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:17.310
So your ability to constrain
that or to check it

00:25:17.310 --> 00:25:20.940
is dramatically changed from
where you've got JavaScript

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:23.370
or something on the server side.

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:25.650
So all of a sudden we've got
a whole new class of ways

00:25:25.650 --> 00:25:30.030
of trying to solve this input
validation or data lineage.

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:32.132
Think of the other thing that,

00:25:32.132 --> 00:25:33.900
how do we know which data
is being used in training?

00:25:33.900 --> 00:25:35.280
How do we know where the data goes

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:36.960
that's entered into the models?

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:38.550
That's what this is designed to do

00:25:38.550 --> 00:25:41.010
is to take the best of the last 20 years

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:44.400
and make sure in the flurry of activity,

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:47.890
we don't forget all these
foundational things,

00:25:47.890 --> 00:25:52.680
things we learned in the
cryptography courses.

00:25:52.680 --> 00:25:53.520
We don't throw those out

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:56.040
just because, you know,
you do have this risk

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:59.790
every time there's a
massive innovative moment

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:03.060
that people build it
from scratch very quickly

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:04.800
to get to market, and they forget about

00:26:04.800 --> 00:26:08.613
all the work we've done over
the years to, on security.

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:13.830
So I hope that's at least a table setting.

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:16.773
And I think the key for me is that,

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:20.190
and the reason I came to Google was

00:26:20.190 --> 00:26:23.700
I could never solve the
problems we're talking about

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:28.700
or participate at one bank or
one, you know, institution.

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:33.540
You had to be part of
this fabric of technology,

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:36.510
whether it was data centers and cloud,

00:26:36.510 --> 00:26:39.660
whether it's mobile
devices, whether it's email

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:42.330
and video rendering.

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:44.040
I think that we're at a unique moment

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:46.470
where the infrastructure
for the 21st century

00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:48.000
is being laid down.

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:53.000
If we mess it up now,
it'll be like the railroads

00:26:53.112 --> 00:26:56.880
of a previous era and
you won't change those,

00:26:56.880 --> 00:26:59.340
the gauge of the railways again.

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:02.040
So we'll live with
whatever infrastructure,

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:04.503
security we build right now for AI.

00:27:06.133 --> 00:27:07.410
I'd love to answer questions.

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:08.648
That'd be great.

00:27:08.648 --> 00:27:10.115
Thanks.

00:27:10.115 --> 00:27:11.653
(audience applauding)

00:27:11.653 --> 00:27:13.800
- I'm sort of fascinated by
the framing you give, Royal,

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:18.600
about the need for a
certain level of scale,

00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:21.690
and I think, you know,
we wrestle with this

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:24.240
in universities as we
do strategic planning

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:28.290
and think about how we can, you know,

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:31.710
we're not gonna be in the
business of competing on scale

00:27:31.710 --> 00:27:34.600
with Google, but we do want
to feel like we're part

00:27:35.829 --> 00:27:37.192
of a conversation that involves leadership

00:27:37.192 --> 00:27:39.291
where the scale exists.

00:27:39.291 --> 00:27:42.420
What do you see the right kind of way

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:44.880
for universities to engage
with these questions,

00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:48.540
which clearly are affecting
and asking questions

00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:51.120
that leaders in academia
should be thinking about?

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:55.980
- Yeah, I mean the good
news is I think on work

00:27:55.980 --> 00:28:00.690
