So now we're I think more open than,

00:28:57.180 --> 00:28:59.440
you know, I don't think 10, 15 years ago

00:29:00.671 --> 00:29:02.970
it would've been even
interesting or relevant

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:05.010
to some of these companies
to be in those discussions.

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:06.033
I think it is now.

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:10.710
How do we bring the other
departments around the university

00:29:10.710 --> 00:29:13.140
to the table in concrete enough ways?

00:29:13.140 --> 00:29:14.790
It struck me, we talked about this before,

00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:17.640
like as the Deans were
all around that room,

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:20.820
just have to have one
conversation in that room,

00:29:20.820 --> 00:29:24.390
but could it become 15 conversations

00:29:24.390 --> 00:29:29.390
about regulation, healthcare,
you know, different areas.

00:29:30.874 --> 00:29:32.760
So I would, I think you can help us

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:35.580
by convening that cross-disciplinary work

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:38.640
in ways that we would
never even know how to.

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:41.360
- Yeah, I'm looking forward to that.

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:44.760
So first of all, and
I think second of all,

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:48.750
it is interesting how
we are already seeing

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:50.850
kind of calls on computer science

00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:54.090
and on the technical side from engineering

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:55.260
from the other schools.

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:58.500
You know, we need not
just to apply algorithms

00:29:58.500 --> 00:30:02.511
to say medical data or problems like this,

00:30:02.511 --> 00:30:04.500
but to really understand mechanistically

00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:06.331
how these things are working

00:30:06.331 --> 00:30:07.852
and how they're processing information.

00:30:07.852 --> 00:30:09.783
So I think that's a key point.

00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:13.860
The other thing though that
sometimes we hear internally

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:18.860
to engineering is, well, you
know, we saw neural networks,

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:21.150
and then there was a long winter,

00:30:21.150 --> 00:30:24.120
and then there was a little
sort of data science blip,

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:26.724
and then there was another long winter,

00:30:26.724 --> 00:30:28.560
and this is just gonna
be another hype cycle,

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:30.210
and there'll be another long AI winter.

00:30:30.210 --> 00:30:32.430
Do you see that as?

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:33.720
- That's a great question

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:35.524
because I, you know,

00:30:35.524 --> 00:30:37.043
I've seen a number of those along the way.

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:39.510
I, you know, just personally

00:30:39.510 --> 00:30:42.650
and the more I work with the
Deep Mind folks and others

00:30:42.650 --> 00:30:45.090
in the kind of work we're talking about,

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:47.580
whether it's for the malware analysis

00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:50.943
or safety filtering on the internet,

00:30:53.851 --> 00:30:57.180
people I really respect
that are not into this

00:30:57.180 --> 00:31:00.630
for the investment side,

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:02.413
'cause I think that, you know,

00:31:02.413 --> 00:31:04.271
that's sort of a,

00:31:04.271 --> 00:31:05.243
it's helpful but not
always the right signal,

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:11.430
are surprised at what this is able to do

00:31:11.430 --> 00:31:13.863
in ways that I think
make me feel like we are,

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:16.873
there's still a lot to go here.

00:31:16.873 --> 00:31:19.233
And just my thinking on it,

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:21.660
just for what it's worth
and talking about it

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:24.270
is that the big difference is gonna be

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:29.270
can these large models or
the next layer up from them

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:32.730
become the equivalent
of the operating system?

00:31:32.730 --> 00:31:34.290
Instead of you going, you know,

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:36.180
all these problems I talked about before,

00:31:36.180 --> 00:31:38.460
you go and solve one use case at a time,

00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:39.900
and you train data,

00:31:39.900 --> 00:31:40.920
and you tune the data,

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:43.920
and then you put it at a
particular point in the logic

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:45.470
of the code to make a decision.

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:51.111
I'm starting to see instances
where the user themselves

00:31:51.111 --> 00:31:56.111
in interacting with that next
layer of, you know, model,

00:31:57.630 --> 00:31:59.733
tune the model as they use it,

00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:03.240
and so you, that'll be far more effective

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:07.500
and efficient in writing software

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:09.750
than having a software developer

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:12.210
in the middle of that activity.

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:14.070
If we can do that, I think you,

00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:15.660
that is a very different world

00:32:15.660 --> 00:32:19.140
than one of a bunch of
Linux boxes strung together

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:20.520
running some middleware.

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:22.509
- [Jeffrey] Right, right.

00:32:22.509 --> 00:32:24.928
- And I think that's, that's
what people are working on.

00:32:24.928 --> 00:32:26.310
- Yeah, that, I mean I think

00:32:26.310 --> 00:32:28.560
that's one of the things we
heard when we were out west

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:29.970
is that this,

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:34.970
this changes the way anyone
can interact with a computer.

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:36.400
Whether it's someone

00:32:37.394 --> 00:32:38.460
who wants to write a
technical piece of code

00:32:38.460 --> 00:32:41.670
or someone who just kind
of wants to take an idea

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:44.691
and turn it into something that
can be deployed technically.

00:32:44.691 --> 00:32:47.251
- I mean the interesting,
and I'm really enjoying this,

00:32:47.251 --> 00:32:48.480
I talk about my, you know,

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:52.588
back to the neural network
with Professor Lerner was that

00:32:52.588 --> 00:32:57.588
you know, so much of the work historically

00:32:57.690 --> 00:33:01.350
went into training, you
know, or into labeling data.

00:33:01.350 --> 00:33:04.950
Obviously the deep
learning quickly allowed us

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:08.010
to label, auto label data
and train these models.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:10.440
But what I didn't realize is
this reinforcement learning

00:33:10.440 --> 00:33:12.480
with human feedback

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:15.120
and different versions of that, you know,

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:18.510
if you can get an expert
to react to the output

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:19.773
of one of these models,

00:33:20.820 --> 00:33:22.950
you can immediately use that feedback

00:33:22.950 --> 00:33:25.860
and the model gets exponentially better,

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:27.310
not just a little bit better.

00:33:28.350 --> 00:33:31.350
And so all of a sudden the user

00:33:31.350 --> 00:33:34.380
becomes the most valuable
piece of the equation.

00:33:34.380 --> 00:33:38.760
It's why I'm actually not
worried about the labor questions

00:33:38.760 --> 00:33:39.593
in many ways.

00:33:39.593 --> 00:33:41.070
I actually think people
are gonna find this

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:44.220
remarkably democratic and democratizing

00:33:44.220 --> 00:33:46.090
'cause they're gonna be able to do things

00:33:46.090 --> 00:33:47.170
they were never able to,

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:49.020
and they in fact will add what they know

00:33:49.020 --> 00:33:50.370
that nobody else knew.

00:33:50.370 --> 00:33:53.040
And the model will get that
much better that quickly.

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:54.780
But that's not the way we write software,

00:33:54.780 --> 00:33:56.371
and that's what,

00:33:56.371 --> 00:33:57.770
that's the work that's gotta be done.

00:33:57.770 --> 00:33:58.774
- Right.

00:33:58.774 --> 00:33:59.790
- Because you can't just say, okay,

00:33:59.790 --> 00:34:00.623
let's use it like an operating system.

00:34:00.623 --> 00:34:02.100
That isn't, like that's not works.

00:34:02.100 --> 00:34:03.570
- Interesting.

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:05.460
We were talking a little bit earlier about

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:09.490
how as products of
Yale, you have a kind of

00:34:10.630 --> 00:34:14.340
a latent humanism that is
kind of baked into your brain

00:34:14.340 --> 00:34:16.140
regardless of what you end up doing.

00:34:17.130 --> 00:34:20.460
What, you know, as we
think about strategically

00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:21.630
what makes the most sense

00:34:21.630 --> 00:34:24.030
for us to be doubling down on here,

00:34:24.030 --> 00:34:26.160
how do you see your own experience at Yale

00:34:26.160 --> 00:34:28.140
as informing your work on the one hand?

00:34:28.140 --> 00:34:33.140
And two, do you think we have
