And I worry that, you know, I
think what we need is societal

00:51:40.470 --> 00:51:42.690
and legal norms that go with that.

00:51:42.690 --> 00:51:45.330
The technical implementation
is not gonna be

00:51:45.330 --> 00:51:49.593
either the answer or the impediment.

00:51:50.550 --> 00:51:53.550
And I think we're gonna need new norms

00:51:53.550 --> 00:51:56.730
of when you modify someone else's,

00:51:56.730 --> 00:51:57.960
not even the technical platform,

00:51:57.960 --> 00:52:00.210
but you modify someone else's material.

00:52:00.210 --> 00:52:01.310
How's that gonna work?

00:52:02.182 --> 00:52:03.183
- [Jeffrey] Right.

00:52:03.183 --> 00:52:04.181
- How will licensing work?

00:52:04.181 --> 00:52:06.360
So I think all of that, back
to the question of regulation

00:52:06.360 --> 00:52:09.005
has to be a whole of society question.

00:52:09.005 --> 00:52:13.710
I think we get in trouble
when, you know, we as engineers

00:52:13.710 --> 00:52:17.007
or we as society think there's
one answer to these things,

00:52:17.007 --> 00:52:20.100
and we've just not found
the mathematical equation.

00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:21.930
These are actually ongoing processes

00:52:21.930 --> 00:52:26.930
that need feedback loops and
healthy, legitimate outcomes

00:52:27.030 --> 00:52:29.070
that people respect

00:52:29.070 --> 00:52:30.170
'cause they'll evolve.

00:52:32.723 --> 00:52:37.723
- [Jeffrey] Yeah, where
there a question over here?

00:52:38.283 --> 00:52:39.840
- [Audience Member] So I
kind of wanna return back

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:44.823
to the idea of like regulation
under industry verticals.

00:52:44.823 --> 00:52:47.190
So something that I'm really interested in

00:52:47.190 --> 00:52:50.100
is that like it seems really
easy for all these companies

00:52:50.100 --> 00:52:52.170
to talk about like these big lofty ideas

00:52:52.170 --> 00:52:54.210
of like we need to be regulated.

00:52:54.210 --> 00:52:56.160
But when it comes down
to the actual language

00:52:56.160 --> 00:52:58.590
of like what these regulations are,

00:52:58.590 --> 00:53:00.180
it becomes really tricky.

00:53:00.180 --> 00:53:02.520
And like the specific
instance that I'm thinking of

00:53:02.520 --> 00:53:06.450
is like when Sam Altman
testified in front of Congress

00:53:06.450 --> 00:53:08.610
and then like saying that
we need more regulation,

00:53:08.610 --> 00:53:11.400
but then a couple days later
the EU came out with a package

00:53:11.400 --> 00:53:14.940
and OpenAI just completely rejected it.

00:53:14.940 --> 00:53:17.100
So do you have any like ideas in mind

00:53:17.100 --> 00:53:19.800
to like how to resolve this
kind of issue that we see?

00:53:20.700 --> 00:53:25.284
- Yeah, and I, again, back
to my banking history,

00:53:25.284 --> 00:53:28.110
I think we need to get to a place

00:53:28.110 --> 00:53:33.110
where there's a healthy and
informed ongoing refinement

00:53:33.820 --> 00:53:35.940
of the approach.

00:53:35.940 --> 00:53:37.560
And so to your point, I
think there's, you know,

00:53:37.560 --> 00:53:39.660
this is new, and people
are posturing a bit

00:53:39.660 --> 00:53:42.000
in all kinds of ways,

00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:47.000
but what we want is industry,
government, this public sector

00:53:48.360 --> 00:53:50.160
in whatever form

00:53:50.160 --> 00:53:53.400
iterating towards something that works.

00:53:53.400 --> 00:53:58.080
I guess my point is I don't
think there's a single group

00:53:58.080 --> 00:54:01.560
within that who knows enough
to write the perfect regulation

00:54:01.560 --> 00:54:03.030
even if it existed.

00:54:03.030 --> 00:54:05.400
And so what we need is a
healthy way, to your point,

00:54:05.400 --> 00:54:07.680
you know, we need a healthy
way to have the dialogue,

00:54:07.680 --> 00:54:10.020
not to sort of reject things out of hand,

00:54:10.020 --> 00:54:11.553
but to refine them as we go.

00:54:12.750 --> 00:54:14.940
Because look, I just said, we talk about,

00:54:14.940 --> 00:54:17.561
I don't know what it'll
look like in a couple years

00:54:17.561 --> 00:54:20.610
talking about a large, a frontier model.

00:54:20.610 --> 00:54:23.430
Will that be, like that's a concept,

00:54:23.430 --> 00:54:24.780
but is it well-defined enough

00:54:24.780 --> 00:54:26.910
to be the basis of a regulation?

00:54:26.910 --> 00:54:28.590
That's a good question.

00:54:28.590 --> 00:54:31.560
And so I think we need to use
that. We need a vocabulary,

00:54:31.560 --> 00:54:34.020
and it's partly why I go
back to the domain specific,

00:54:34.020 --> 00:54:35.910
because again, when we operate

00:54:35.910 --> 00:54:38.850
in these really high level abstractions,

00:54:38.850 --> 00:54:43.850
I'm not sure that it's even
implementable in some cases,

00:54:44.220 --> 00:54:47.730
let alone, you know, to
people's reactions to it.

00:54:47.730 --> 00:54:50.670
Whereas you can look at
outcomes in a domain,

00:54:50.670 --> 00:54:52.670
even if the tech changes under the hood.

00:54:55.260 --> 00:54:56.827
- All right, I think we have time

00:54:56.827 --> 00:54:57.660
for maybe one more,

00:55:01.470 --> 00:55:02.790
and if there're none, I have one,

00:55:02.790 --> 00:55:04.626
and you knew it was coming.

00:55:04.626 --> 00:55:05.988
Right here?

00:55:05.988 --> 00:55:10.847
- [Audience Member] Yes, thank
you, Royal, your analogy,

00:55:15.209 --> 00:55:17.010
- Oh, we need a mic for you, I guess.

00:55:17.010 --> 00:55:18.210
I can hear you, but yep.

00:55:19.846 --> 00:55:21.810
- [Audience Member] I enjoyed
your analogy with cartography,

00:55:21.810 --> 00:55:23.913
the definition of a country,

00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:30.500
and it made me think of
digital and data sovereignty.

00:55:32.548 --> 00:55:33.730
- Yeah.

00:55:33.730 --> 00:55:36.990
- [Audience Member] Is it correct to think

00:55:36.990 --> 00:55:41.990
that it's not too late to
define a sovereign nation

00:55:42.780 --> 00:55:44.823
with respect to data and digital,

00:55:45.807 --> 00:55:50.807
and if so, what criteria
would you, can you think of,

00:55:51.240 --> 00:55:55.413
what major high level
criteria would you focus on?

00:55:56.310 --> 00:55:58.440
- You know, back to the crypto discussion,

00:55:58.440 --> 00:56:01.506
we'll come back to my education here,

00:56:01.506 --> 00:56:05.340
but we have built just as one example

00:56:05.340 --> 00:56:07.173
of an attempt on this,

00:56:08.388 --> 00:56:10.020
what we call a sovereign cloud offering

00:56:11.043 --> 00:56:13.350
where instead of it
being, you know, we do,

00:56:13.350 --> 00:56:16.410
you can do it in various locations,

00:56:16.410 --> 00:56:20.070
but we use crypto to define the scope.

00:56:20.070 --> 00:56:24.510
And so the cryptography defines
the sovereignty completely.

00:56:24.510 --> 00:56:29.510
Really it's geographically
meaningless really,

00:56:30.540 --> 00:56:32.760
but it gives the basis for discussion

00:56:32.760 --> 00:56:35.850
of this, the scope of the data.

00:56:35.850 --> 00:56:37.290
And I think you still
have questions though

00:56:37.290 --> 00:56:38.640
of what data,

00:56:38.640 --> 00:56:43.640
is any data about me because
I'm an American, American data?

00:56:44.185 --> 00:56:46.980
You know, I think there's
lots of use cases,

00:56:46.980 --> 00:56:49.410
but at least if somebody can say,

00:56:49.410 --> 00:56:53.040
this is what we wanna consider
the data of that country,

00:56:53.040 --> 00:56:55.440
I think cryptographic work

00:56:55.440 --> 00:56:58.050
provides one possibility
for how to answer that.

00:56:58.050 --> 00:57:00.790
The problem is, the practicality is

00:57:00.790 --> 00:57:02.580
no data lives on its own.

00:57:02.580 --> 00:57:03.750
I mean very little.

00:57:03.750 --> 00:57:06.000
It needs to interact with other data.

00:57:06.000 --> 00:57:08.880
And so if you're using crypto to do it,

00:57:08.880 --> 00:57:13.108
you need some key exchange or
some means of combining data,

00:57:13.108 --> 00:57:16.011
and all of a sudden you
make people manage keys,

00:57:16.011 --> 00:57:17.703
and people stink at that.

00:57:19.186 --> 00:57:21.990
So yes, there's some offerings,

00:57:21.990 --> 00:57:23.540
and I think crypto provides it.

00:57:24.480 --> 00:57:25.440
I don't think that's the
long-term answer though.

00:57:25.440 --> 00:57:27.406
I think we've gotta come up with

00:57:27.406 --> 00:57:31.650
more durable conceptual
models of what the country,

00:57:31.650 --> 00:57:36.266
its people, its companies,
its technical infrastructure.

00:57:36.266 --> 00:57:38.310
But I think that's work
I'd love to be doing.

00:57:38.310 --> 00:57:39.660
I mean, I honestly think that's a question

00:57:39.660 --> 00:57:43.920
that the university should
begin to have a thesis about,

00:57:43.920 --> 00:57:46.050
not necessarily Google.

00:57:46.050 --> 00:57:49.291
- [Jeffrey] Yeah, So last question.

00:57:49.291 --> 00:57:50.124
- So I think

00:57:50.124 --> 00:57:51.840
- Michael has one?

00:57:51.840 --> 00:57:53.010
We gotta give Michael the mic.

00:57:53.010 --> 00:57:53.843
- Yes.
- Yes.

00:57:53.843 --> 00:57:56.040
- [Michael] Okay.

00:57:56.040 --> 00:57:56.873
- Turn it on.

00:57:57.960 --> 00:58:00.363
It's on, okay, fine, great.

00:58:00.363 --> 00:58:05.363
- So I just wanted to
say that it's very easy

00:58:06.030 --> 00:58:10.230
for technologists to think about,

00:58:10.230 --> 00:58:13.890
we have to reach out
into these other areas

00:58:13.890 --> 00:58:17.378
and incorporate law and medicine

00:58:17.378 --> 00:58:21.180
and political science into our thinking

00:58:21.180 --> 00:58:23.460
and into our technologies.

00:58:23.460 --> 00:58:25.350
But I think you made a good point

00:58:25.350 --> 00:58:29.070
that it's the players in those fields

00:58:29.070 --> 00:58:32.760
that are going to be
determining our future.

00:58:32.760 --> 00:58:35.050
And my question is

00:58:36.390 --> 00:58:40.080
can you give any advice to universities

00:58:40.080 --> 00:58:45.080
about how we can best get
the technological foundations

00:58:49.500 --> 00:58:50.640
out to the people

00:58:50.640 --> 00:58:52.770
that are going to be
making these decisions?

00:58:52.770 --> 00:58:56.070
In other words, what should we be doing

00:58:56.070 --> 00:59:01.070
to help our related colleagues

00:59:02.910 --> 00:59:07.200
better understand the
technology and where it's going

00:59:07.200 --> 00:59:08.900
and how it's going to affect them?

00:59:09.960 --> 00:59:12.560
- It's a great question,

00:59:12.560 --> 00:59:15.420
and I think the tension is,
I mean, I think the best way,

00:59:15.420 --> 00:59:18.620
and we come back to I
will keep doing this,

00:59:18.620 --> 00:59:23.620
is concrete questions that
we can work on together.

00:59:23.880 --> 00:59:27.570
Because I think you learn then,
not just the theory of it,

00:59:27.570 --> 00:59:29.880
but the practice.

00:59:29.880 --> 00:59:31.440
And I think that's what is required

00:59:31.440 --> 00:59:33.750
to really learn and to
develop that abstraction

00:59:33.750 --> 00:59:35.550
and language that we can then use

00:59:35.550 --> 00:59:38.070
for the next instance
and the next instance.

00:59:38.070 --> 00:59:41.040
So I think concrete work
where we answer questions

00:59:41.040 --> 00:59:43.680
like what's a country on the internet?

00:59:43.680 --> 00:59:46.230
And then we force each other to learn,

00:59:46.230 --> 00:59:50.162
well, what do you mean, a data center?

00:59:50.162 --> 00:59:52.440
Like at some level people don't
really understand what that

00:59:53.610 --> 00:59:58.610
or how caching works, right?

00:59:58.677 --> 01:00:00.900
And I think you get into
these sort of concepts,

01:00:00.900 --> 01:00:01.743
concrete things.

01:00:02.842 --> 01:00:03.860
The one thing I would say,

01:00:03.860 --> 01:00:05.280
and this is one thing I love about Yale,

01:00:05.280 --> 01:00:10.050
I do worry sometimes if the
over enthusiasm for data,

01:00:10.050 --> 01:00:12.139
so one way this all happens

01:00:12.139 --> 01:00:13.470
is everybody gets excited
about data, right?

01:00:13.470 --> 01:00:15.120
All these fields are now quantitative

01:00:15.120 --> 01:00:18.622
in ways that seem
ridiculous to me somewhat.

01:00:18.622 --> 01:00:22.963
But, so I think that the key is not to be,

01:00:24.541 --> 01:00:27.180
not to think of data as,

01:00:27.180 --> 01:00:29.464
data is a way to communicate.

01:00:29.464 --> 01:00:30.840
It is not the ideas in and of itself.

01:00:30.840 --> 01:00:33.243
So I think one of the
things the university

01:00:33.243 --> 01:00:34.620
has to continue to offer is to remind us

01:00:34.620 --> 01:00:37.230
that these ideas are longer standing

01:00:37.230 --> 01:00:40.080
and these concepts go back much further.

01:00:40.080 --> 01:00:42.130
The data is a way to have a conversation.

01:00:43.082 --> 01:00:45.373
It is not the answer. It
does not solve the conflict.

01:00:45.373 --> 01:00:47.730
It does not solve the
partisanship in and of itself.

01:00:47.730 --> 01:00:49.421
So that's my,

01:00:49.421 --> 01:00:50.880
I guess my one back and
forth on the advice is,

01:00:50.880 --> 01:00:52.559
yeah, let's work on things.

01:00:52.559 --> 01:00:53.392
Sometimes that means people get data,

01:00:53.392 --> 01:00:54.390
and they work together.

01:00:54.390 --> 01:00:57.270
I worry that sometimes that, you know,

01:00:57.270 --> 01:00:59.090
stops people from digging in

01:00:59.090 --> 01:01:02.073
at the real roots of these problems.

01:01:03.261 --> 01:01:07.380
- Yeah, well, I wanna thank
Royal again for joining us

01:01:07.380 --> 01:01:09.685
and enlightening us.

01:01:09.685 --> 01:01:10.841
(audience applauding)

01:01:10.841 --> 01:01:11.841
- Thank you.

01:01:13.920 --> 01:01:17.370
- One of the things I loved about his talk

01:01:17.370 --> 01:01:20.850
and about this conversation
is it kind of reaffirms,

01:01:20.850 --> 01:01:24.750
I think, a belief that we share here

01:01:24.750 --> 01:01:27.120
that there's work going on in the world

01:01:27.120 --> 01:01:28.740
and technology companies

01:01:28.740 --> 01:01:30.870
and lots of different places

01:01:30.870 --> 01:01:33.210
that should be in deep conversation

01:01:33.210 --> 01:01:35.640
with what we're doing here in academia.

01:01:35.640 --> 01:01:38.370
The research we're doing,
the teaching we're doing,

01:01:38.370 --> 01:01:41.200
that walling ourselves
off from these advances

01:01:42.090 --> 01:01:45.600
does us and society no good.

01:01:45.600 --> 01:01:49.470
So I'm thrilled that there
seems to be mutual enthusiasm

01:01:49.470 --> 01:01:51.120
for continuing this conversation,

01:01:51.120 --> 01:01:54.270
and I wanna thank you all
for joining us here today.

01:01:54.270 --> 01:01:55.500
So more next semester.

01:01:55.500 --> 01:01:56.904
Thanks again.

01:01:56.904 --> 01:01:57.903
- Thank you.

01:01:57.903 --> 01:02:01.070
(audience applauding)

01:02:05.230 --> 01:02:07.980
(soothing tones)

